            <ArticleTitle>Reaction to a surgical implant foreign body masquerading as recurrent uterine sarcoma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>450-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/AOG.0b013e3181f70bf0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Multiple products to prevent adhesions or lessen the risk of soft tissue attachments are commercially available. The long-term nature of these products is unknown, and they may cause foreign body reactions masquerading as recurrent disease in patients with cancer.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE" NlmCategory="METHODS">A perimenopausal female underwent a hysterectomy and placement of a surgical implant, polylactic acid. Final pathology revealed stage IA low-grade endometrial stromal sarcoma. Areas suspicious for recurrence were noted on radiographic imaging 1 year later, resulting in exploratory surgery. The suspicious areas were found to be foreign body reactions. Mass spectrometry identified the main component of the reactions as polylactic acid.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Adhesion barriers and other surgical implants may not always be completely metabolized and should be used with caution in patients with cancer.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
